It is important to emphasize that the indices of the vertices of the graph \(A_{n}\) take modulo \(n\). For example, the graph of convex polytope \(A_{7}\), is displayed in Figure 1.

Theorem 1. For every \(n \geqslant 5, A_{n}\) is a \(d\)-balanced graph.
Proof. The vertices of \(A_{n}\) are distributed over three layers, each containing \(n\) vertices. Within each layer, the vertices form a cycle of length \(n\). The adjacency between the first and second layers follows the same pattern as the one between the second and third layers, while no edges connecting any vertex from the first layer with any vertex from the third layers. Thus, the \(3 n \times 3 n\) format matrix of the linear operator \(M\left(A_{n}\right)\) can be represented as a block matrix composed of the nine \(n \times n\) layer-wise adjacency matrices. More precisely, the matrix of the linear operator \(M\left(A_{n}\right)\) can be written in a block form as:
\[
M\left(A_{n}\right)=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
C & B & O \\
B^{T} & C & B \\
O & B^{T} & C
\end{array}\right],
\]
where \(C:=\left[\begin{array}{ccccccc}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 & \ldots & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 & \ldots & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 & \ldots & 0 & 0 \\ \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots \\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \ldots & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \ldots & 1 & 1\end{array}\right]\) is the \(n \times n\) matrix of a cycle graph,
describing edges within each layer, \(B:=\left[\begin{array}{ccccccc}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 & \ldots & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & \ldots & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & \ldots & 0 & 0 \\ \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \vdots \\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \ldots & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \ldots & 0 & 1\end{array}\right]\) is the
\(n \times n\) adjacency matrix describing edges between consecutive layers, \(B^{T}\) is its transpose matrix, while \(O\) denotes the \(n \times n\) zero matrix.

To prove that \(A_{n}\) is a \(d\)-balanced graph, it is sufficient to show that every vector \(\mathbf{x}=\left(x_{1}, \ldots, x_{3 n}\right) \in \operatorname{ker}\left(M\left(A_{n}\right)\right)\) satisfies the condition \(\sum_{i=1}^{3 n} x_{i}=0\).

Let \(\mathbf{x}=\left(x_{1}, \ldots, x_{3 n}\right) \in \operatorname{ker}\left(M\left(A_{n}\right)\right)\) be an arbitrary vector. We partition this vector layer-wise into three subvectors \(\mathbf{x}_{1}, \mathbf{x}_{2}\) and \(\mathbf{x}_{3}\), corresponding to the layers of the graph \(A_{n}\) :
subvector \(\mathbf{x}_{1}:=\left(x_{1}, \ldots, x_{n}\right)\) corresponding to the first layer,
subvector \(\mathbf{x}_{2}:=\left(x_{n+1}, \ldots, x_{2 n}\right)\) corresponding to the second layer,
subvector \(\mathbf{x}_{3}:=\left(x_{2 n+1}, \ldots, x_{3 n}\right)\) corresponding to the third layer.
Moreover, let us define three sums of the components of these subvectors: \(s_{1}:=\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_{i}, s_{2}:=\sum_{i=n+1}^{2 n} x_{i}\) and \(s_{3}:=\sum_{i=2 n+1}^{3 n} x_{i}\). Obviously, it holds \(\sum_{i=1}^{3 n} x_{i}=s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}\).

Using the previous notation, the system of the linear equations \(M\left(A_{n}\right) \cdot\) \(\mathbf{x}^{T}=\mathbf{0}^{T}\) is stated as \(\left[\begin{array}{ccc}C & B & O \\ B^{T} & C & B \\ O & B^{T} & C\end{array}\right] \cdot\left[\begin{array}{l}\mathbf{x}_{1}^{T} \\ \mathbf{x}_{2}^{T} \\ \mathbf{x}_{3}^{T}\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{l}\mathbf{0}^{T} \\ \mathbf{0}^{T} \\ \mathbf{0}^{T}\end{array}\right]\), where each of the \(\mathbf{0}\) on the right side is a \(n \times 1\) row vector. This leads to the system of equations
\[
\begin{aligned}
& C \cdot \mathbf{x}_{1}^{T}+B \cdot \mathbf{x}_{2}^{T}=\mathbf{0}^{T} \\
& B^{T} \cdot \mathbf{x}_{1}^{T}+C \cdot \mathbf{x}_{2}^{T}+B \cdot \mathbf{x}_{3}^{T}=\mathbf{0}^{T} \\
& B^{T} \cdot \mathbf{x}_{2}^{T}+C \cdot \mathbf{x}_{3}^{T}=\mathbf{0}^{T}
\end{aligned}
\]
Multiply each equation in this system from the left by the \(1 \times n\) row vector \(1:=(1, \ldots, 1)\). Since every column of the matrix \(C\) contains exactly three
ones and every column and every row of the matrix \(B\) contains exactly two ones, we conclude \(\mathbf{1} \cdot C=3 \cdot \mathbf{1}\) and \(\mathbf{1} \cdot B=\mathbf{1} \cdot B^{T}=2 \cdot \mathbf{1}\). Also, it holds \(\mathbf{1} \cdot \mathbf{x}_{i}^{T}=s_{i}, i \in\{1,2,3\}\). Hence, the previous system reduces to the following system of linear equations
\[
\begin{aligned}
& 3 s_{1}+2 s_{2}=0 \\
& 2 s_{1}+3 s_{2}+2 s_{3}=0 \\
& 2 s_{2}+3 s_{3}=0
\end{aligned}
\]
From the first equation, we have \(s_{1}=-\frac{2}{3} s_{2}\) and from the third equation \(s_{3}=\) \(-\frac{2}{3} s_{2}\). Substituting these values into the second equation gives \(\frac{1}{3} s_{2}=0\), that is \(s_{2}=0\), and consequently \(s_{1}=s_{3}=0\). Therefore, \(\sum_{i=1}^{3 n} x_{i}=s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}=0\), which concludes the proof. \(\square\)

The "layer-sum technique" from the previous theorem extends naturally to a general framework for establishing \(d\)-balancedness. Let \(G\) be a graph whose vertex set \(V(G)\) is partitioned into \(k \geqslant 2\) layers such that each block of \(M(G)\) has a constant column sum \(c_{i j}\). Any vector \(\mathbf{x} \in \operatorname{ker}(M(G))\) splits layer-wise into subvectors \(\mathbf{x}_{1}, \ldots, \mathbf{x}_{k}\) with component sums \(s_{1}, \ldots, s_{k}\). Leftmultiplying each block row in \(M(G) \mathbf{x}^{T}=\mathbf{0}^{T}\) by the appropriate all-ones row vector yields the reduced system \(\sum_{j=1}^{k} c_{i j} s_{j}=0, i \in\{1, \ldots, k\}\). If every solution of this reduced system satisfies \(\sum_{i=1}^{k} s_{i}=0\), then all vectors in \(\operatorname{ker}(M(G))\) have zero component sum, proving that \(G\) is \(d\)-balanced.

In the next two theorems, we apply the technique described above. Their proofs focus primarily on the inter-layer connection types specific to the graph classes under consideration, while omitting the routine computational details common to all cases.

The convex polytope \(D_{n}, n \geqslant 5\), was introduced in [28]. Formally, graph \(D_{n}=\left(V\left(D_{n}\right), E\left(D_{n}\right)\right)\) consists of the following sets of vertices and edges:
\[
\begin{aligned}
& V\left(D_{n}\right)=\left\{u_{i}, v_{i}, w_{i}, q_{i} \mid 0 \leqslant i \leqslant n-1\right\} \\
& E\left(D_{n}\right)=\left\{u_{i} u_{i+1}, q_{i} q_{i+1}, u_{i} v_{i}, v_{i} w_{i}, v_{i+1} w_{i}, w_{i} q_{i} \mid 0 \leqslant i \leqslant n-1\right\} .
\end{aligned}
\]
We emphasize that the indices of the vertices of \(D_{n}\) are taken modulo \(n\). For example, the graph of convex polytope \(D_{7}\) is shown in Figure 2.

Theorem 2. For every \(n \geqslant 5, D_{n}\) is a \(d\)-balanced graph.

