Proof. The vertices of \(D_{n}\) are arranged into four layers \(\left\{u_{i}\right\},\left\{v_{i}\right\},\left\{w_{i}\right\}\) and \(\left\{q_{i}\right\}\), each containing \(n\) vertices. In terms of graph connectivity, the column sums \(c_{i j}\) of the matrix blocks correspond to the degrees of adjacencies within and between the layers:
\begin{itemize}
\item[-] 1st layer \(\left(u_{i}\right): c_{11}=2+1=3\) (cycle edges + self), \(c_{12}=1\left(u_{i} v_{i}\right)\).
\item[-] 2nd layer \(\left(v_{i}\right): c_{21}=1\left(u_{i} v_{i}\right), c_{22}=1\) (only self), \(c_{23}=2\left(v_{i} w_{i}, v_{i+1} w_{i}\right)\).
\item[-] 3rd layer \(\left(w_{i}\right): c_{32}=2\left(v_{i} w_{i}, v_{i+1} w_{i}\right), c_{33}=1\) (only self), \(c_{34}=1\left(w_{i} q_{i}\right)\).
\item[-] 4th layer \(\left(q_{i}\right): c_{43}=1\left(w_{i} q_{i}\right), c_{44}=2+1=3\) (cycle edges + self).
\end{itemize}
Consequently, the \(4 n \times 4 n\) matrix of the linear operator \(M\left(D_{n}\right)\) can be written in a block form as:
\[
M\left(D_{n}\right)=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
C & I & O & O \\
I & I & B & O \\
O & B^{T} & I & I \\
O & O & I & C
\end{array}\right],
\]
where \(C, B, O\) are the same \(n \times n\) matrices as in the proof of the previous theorem and \(I\) is the \(n \times n\) identity matrix.

Let \(\mathbf{x}=\left(x_{1}, \ldots, x_{4 n}\right) \in \operatorname{ker}\left(M\left(D_{n}\right)\right)\) be an arbitrary vector. We partition this vector layer-wise into four subvectors \(\mathbf{x}_{k}:=\left(x_{(k-1) \cdot n+1}, \ldots, x_{k \cdot n}\right), k \in\) \(\{1, \ldots, 4\}\), corresponding to the \(k\)-th layer of graph \(D_{n}\), with corresponding component sum \(s_{k}\).

The system of linear equations \(M\left(D_{n}\right) \cdot \mathbf{x}^{T}=\mathbf{0}^{T}\) and corresponding reduced system take the following form
\[
\begin{array}{lr}
C \cdot \mathbf{x}_{1}^{T}+I \cdot \mathbf{x}_{2}^{T}=\mathbf{0}^{T} & 3 s_{1}+s_{2}=0 \\
I \cdot \mathbf{x}_{1}^{T}+I \cdot \mathbf{x}_{2}^{T}+B \cdot \mathbf{x}_{3}^{T}=\mathbf{0}^{T} & s_{1}+s_{2}+2 s_{3}=0 \\
B^{T} \cdot \mathbf{x}_{2}^{T}+I \cdot \mathbf{x}_{3}^{T}+I \cdot \mathbf{x}_{4}^{T}=\mathbf{0}^{T} & 2 s_{2}+s_{3}+s_{4}=0 \\
I \cdot \mathbf{x}_{3}^{T}+C \cdot \mathbf{x}_{4}^{T}=\mathbf{0}^{T}, & s_{3}+3 s_{4}=0 .
\end{array}
\]
The unique solution of the reduced system is \(\left(s_{1}, s_{2}, s_{3}, s_{4}\right)=(0,0,0,0)\). Therefore, \(\sum_{i=1}^{4 n} x_{i}=s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}+s_{4}=0\), which concludes the proof. \(\square\)

The graph of convex polytope \(R_{n}^{\prime \prime}=\left(V\left(R_{n}^{\prime \prime}\right), E\left(R_{n}^{\prime \prime}\right)\right), n \geqslant 5\), was introduced in [29]. It consists of the following sets of vertices and edges:
\[
\begin{aligned}
& V\left(R_{n}^{\prime \prime}\right)=\left\{u_{i}, v_{i}, w_{i}, p_{i}, q_{i}, r_{i} \mid 0 \leqslant i \leqslant n-1\right\} \\
& E\left(R_{n}^{\prime \prime}\right)=\left\{u_{i} u_{i+1}, r_{i} r_{i+1}, u_{i} v_{i}, v_{i} w_{i}, w_{i} p_{i}, p_{i} q_{i}, q_{i} r_{i}, v_{i+1} w_{i}, p_{i} q_{i+1} \mid 0 \leqslant i \leqslant n-1\right\} .
\end{aligned}
\]

We emphasize that the indices of the vertices of \(R_{n}^{\prime \prime}\) are taken modulo \(n\). For example, the graph of convex polytope \(R_{7}^{\prime \prime}\) is shown in Figure 3.

Theorem 3. For every \(n \geqslant 5, R_{n}^{\prime \prime}\) is a \(d\)-balanced graph.
Proof. The vertices of the graph \(R_{n}^{\prime \prime}\) are arranged into six layers \(\left\{u_{i}\right\},\left\{v_{i}\right\}\), \(\left\{w_{i}\right\},\left\{p_{i}\right\},\left\{q_{i}\right\}\) and \(\left\{r_{i}\right\}\), each containing \(n\) vertices. In terms of graph connectivity, the column sums \(c_{i j}\) of the matrix blocks correspond to the degrees of adjacencies within and between the layers:
\begin{itemize}
\item[-] 1st layer \(\left(u_{i}\right): c_{11}=2+1=3\) (cycle edges + self), \(c_{12}=1\left(u_{i} v_{i}\right)\).
\item[-] 2nd layer \(\left(v_{i}\right): c_{21}=1, c_{22}=1\) (only self), \(c_{23}=2\left(v_{i} w_{i}, v_{i+1} w_{i}\right)\).
\item[-] 3rd layer \(\left(w_{i}\right): c_{32}=2, c_{33}=1\) (only self), \(c_{34}=1\left(w_{i} p_{i}\right)\).
\item[-] 4th layer \(\left(p_{i}\right): c_{43}=1, c_{44}=1\) (only self), \(c_{45}=2\left(p_{i} q_{i}, p_{i} q_{i+1}\right)\).
\item[-] 5th layer \(\left(q_{i}\right): c_{54}=2, c_{55}=1\) (only self), \(c_{56}=1\left(q_{i} r_{i}\right)\).
\item[-] 6th layer \(\left(r_{i}\right): c_{65}=1, c_{66}=2+1=3\) (cycle edges + self).
\end{itemize}
Consequently, the \(6 n \times 6 n\) matrix of the linear operator \(M\left(R_{n}^{\prime \prime}\right)\) can be written in a block form as:
\[
M\left(R_{n}^{\prime \prime}\right)=\left[\begin{array}{cccccc}
C & I & O & O & O & O \\
I & I & B & O & O & O \\
O & B^{T} & I & I & O & O \\
O & O & I & I & B & O \\
O & O & O & B^{T} & I & I \\
O & O & O & O & I & C
\end{array}\right],
\]

